<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Lixus</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 25 BCE (approx.) · capital Lixus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An ancient Phoenician and Punic port on the Atlantic coast of Morocco, linked in Greek myth to the Garden of the Hesperides.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lixus lay on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loukkos">Loukkos</a> river near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larache">Larache</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> believed it was among the oldest Phoenician foundations, with a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melqart">Melqart</a> older than that of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gadir">Gades</a>. Archaeology shows Phoenician settlers there from about the 8th century BCE, trading with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> peoples of the interior.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lixus was a self-governing town with Phoenician gods and Punic language, part of the trading world of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>. Its economy rested on fishing, fish-salting and garum production, and it traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, gold and grain along the Atlantic coast. Greek writers placed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garden-of-the-hesperides">Garden of the Hesperides</a> and the struggle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antaeus">Antaeus</a> nearby.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> Lixus came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Kingdom of Mauretania</a>, minting coins with Punic legends, and was fully absorbed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a> around 25 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> later made it a Roman colony, and its ruins, with large fish-salting works, are an archaeological site.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 800 BCE – 25 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:43.78%;width:15.42%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 25 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lixus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Phoenician religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Morocco</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Liks</dd></dl></aside></div>
